(d) Each day a violation continues shall constitute a separate offense.
(e) The remedies provided by this article shall be cumulative and in addition to any other remedies authorized by this Code or applicable law.
(Ord. No. 2026-16, § 1(Exh. A), 6-15-26)

433:
(a) All written rental or lease agreements for residential dwelling units located within the city entered into on or after the effective date of this ordinance shall include the following provision, either verbatim or substantially similar in meaning: The landlord warrants that the premises are fit for human habitation, as required by Georgia law, including O.C.G.A. § 44-7-2(b), as amended by House Bill 404 (2024).
(b) "Fit for human habitation" under this city's Code of Ordinances means that a dwelling unit and its premises are in a condition that does not endanger the life, health, safety, or welfare of the occupants, and that provides the essential physical elements necessary for safe and sanitary living. A dwelling unit is considered fit for human habitation if it meets all the following minimum requirements:
(1) The roof, walls, floors, foundation, and all exterior surfaces are reasonably weather-tight, free from unsafe conditions, and structurally sound;
(2) The dwelling has adequate plumbing facilities in good working order, including: (i) A functional toilet, sink, and bathtub or shower, (ii) hot and cold running water; and (iii) proper sewage disposal;
(3) The dwelling has a safe and functional heating system capable of maintaining an indoor temperature of at least sixty-eight (68) degrees Farenheit in the winter, and, where required by code, a cooling system for extreme summer heat;
(4) The dwelling has an electrical system that is safe, functional, and free from hazards such as exposed wiring, overloaded circuits, or inoperable outlets;
(5) The dwelling has an adequate and potable water supply connected to a municipal or approved private source;
(6) Adequate ventilation is provided through operable windows, fans, or mechanical systems; the unit is free from excessive dampness, mold, or other environmental hazards;
(7) The premises are free from infestations of rodents, insects, or other vermin;
(8) The kitchen and bathroom facilities are clean, operable, and capable of maintaining health standards;
(9) The dwelling contains functioning smoke detectors and, where required, carbon monoxide detectors;
(10) The premises have safe and functional doors, locks, and exits that allow for emergency egress; and
(11) The dwelling complies with all applicable provisions of the city's property maintenance, building, housing, health, and safety codes.
(c) The failure of a written lease to include this provision shall not invalidate the lease nor waive any rights of the tenant under state law, including the implied warranty of habitability. This section is intended to serve as a notification and transparency requirement, not to create or limit any legal remedies.
(d) A violation of this ordinance shall be considered a civil infraction and subject to penalties under section 1-8 of this Code. Each failure to include the required language or provide notice for a separate lease agreement shall constitute a separate offense.
(e) Enforcement shall be administered by the department of inspections, code enforcement, or other designated agency of the city.
Ord. No. 2025-20, § 2, 9-15-25)

440:
The mayor and council hereby find that there is a need to protect the health, safety and general welfare of residents of the city living in multi-family housing furnished to them through payment of money to the owner of the property or management company. The purposes of this division include:
(1) To maintain the quality and stability of multi-family rental housing;
(2) To correct and prevent conditions that adversely affect, or are likely to adversely affect the life, safety, welfare and health of occupants of multi-family rental housing;
(3) To provide minimum standards necessary for the health and safety of the occupants of multi-family rental housing;
(4) To ensure rental units meet all applicable building, fire, health, and safety codes;
(5) To provide standards of maintenance of multi-family rental housing to prevent blight and slums; and
(6) To preserve the value of land and buildings throughout the city.
No requirements set forth in this division should be construed to be considered an apartment registration program as stated under O.C.G.A. § 36-74-30.
It is not the city's intent to intrude upon the fair and accepted contractual relationship between tenant and landlord. The city does not intend to intervene as an advocate of either party, or to act as an arbiter, or to be receptive to the complaints of a tenant or landlord not specifically and clearly relevant to the provisions of this division. In the absence of such relevancy with regard to rental disputes, it is intended that the contracting parties exercise such legal rights as are available to them without the intervention of the city.
The provisions of this division are in addition to, not in lieu of, other applicable standard codes, including, but not limited to, International Property Maintenance Code, International Building Code and International Fire Code, as adopted by the city.
(Ord. No. 2020-12, § 1(Exh. A), 8-3-20)

474:
