496:
(a) No plumbing fixture shall be installed within the city which:
(1) Employs a gravity tank type, flush-a-meter valve, or flush-a-meter tank toilet that uses more than an average of 1.28 gallons of water per flush; provided, however, this paragraph shall not be applicable to one-piece toilets until July 1, 1992;
(2) Employs a shower head that allows a flow of more than 2.0 gallons of water per minute at eighty (80) pounds per square inch of pressure and is listed to the EPA WaterSense Specification for Showerheads;
(3) Employs a urinal that uses more than an average of 0.5 gallons of water per flush;
(4) Employs a lavatory faucet or lavatory replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 1.2 gallons of water per minute at a pressure of sixty (60) pounds per square inch and is listed to the EPA WaterSense High Efficiency Lavatory Faucet Specification; or
(5) Employs a kitchen faucet or kitchen replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 1.8 gallons of water per minute at a pressure of sixty (60) pounds per square inch and conforms to the applicable requirements of ASME A112.18.1/CSA B125.1; provided, however, that such faucets may temporarily increase flow to a maximum of 2.2 gallons per minute but shall return to a maximum flow rate of 1.8 gallons per minute upon valve closure.
(b) The flow restriction device in a shower head must be a permanent and integral part of the shower head and must not be removable to allow flow rates in excess of that stated in subsection (a)(2) above.
(c) Lavatory faucets located in restrooms intended for use by the general public shall be of the metering or self-closing type, in addition to the flow requirements listed in subsection (a)(4) above and shall not exceed 0.25 gallons per metering cycle or 0.5 gallons per minute for non-metering faucets, as applicable.
(Ord. No. 91-2, § 1, 2-18-91; Ord. No. 2026-09, § 3(Exh. C), 5-4-26)

498:
